WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.990
YUFENG GUO: The only thing
better than data is big data.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.039
But getting your hands on large
data sets is no easy feat.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.920
From unwieldy storage
options to difficulty

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.330
getting analytics tools to run
over your dataset properly,

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.570
large data sets can lead
to all sorts of struggles

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.740
when it comes to actually doing
something useful with them.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.280
So what's a data
scientist to do?

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.780
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.500
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.990
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.150
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.410
we're going to check out
the BigQuery public datasets

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.610
and explore the amazing
world of open data.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.290
We all love data, preferably
the more the merrier.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.850
But as file sizes grow
and complexity increases,

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.760
it becomes increasingly
challenging to make

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.650
practical use of that data.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:57.110
BigQuery public datasets are
datasets that Google BigQuery

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:00.330
hosts for you to
access and integrate

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.990
into your applications.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.379
So this means that Google
pays for the storage

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.920
of these datasets and provides
public access to the data

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.080
via your cloud project.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.670
You pay only for the queries
that you perform on the data.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.280
Moreover, there
is a one-terabyte

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.150
per month free tier, making
getting started super easy.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.560
Looking at the
public datasets page,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.510
we can see that at the
time of this recording,

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:29.350
there are nearly
40 public datasets.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.860
Each dataset, in
turn, has many tables.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.290
Thousands of queries
from hundreds of projects

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.420
all over the world
are already making use

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.270
of these vast public datasets.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.450
What's really neat, though,
is that each of these datasets

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.360
comes with a bit
of explanatory text

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.150
that helps you get started
with querying the data

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.590
and understanding its structure.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.510
Let's take a look at example.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.990
Here's the New York
City tree census.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.030
The page shows us
how we can easily

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.760
find the answers
to questions like,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.670
what are the most common tree
species in New York City?

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.780
And how have the tree
species changed since 1995

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.190
in New York City?

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.010
These are all accessible
by literally one click

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.140
from the docs page,
which opens right

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.310
into the BigQuery interface.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.370
Another data set
that is quite amazing

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.530
is the Open Images dataset.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.530
It contains approximately
nine million URLs,

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.690
along with metadata,
for images that

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:31.450
have been annotated with labels
spanning over 6,000 categories.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.690
Using it, you can find answers
to your most pressing questions

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:38.650
about images on the web, like
how many images of a trolley

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.410
bus are in this dataset?

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.430
It's over 3,000.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.460
But I digress.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.840
BigQuery open datasets
is a great way

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.140
to explore public datasets and
practice your data analysis

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.310
skills.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.010
Combined with tools that we've
looked at before, like Data

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.640
Lab, Facets, and TensorFlow,
you could really come up

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.650
with some awesome data science.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.020
So what are you waiting for?

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.330
Head on over to the
Public Datasets page

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.190
and let your analysis run wild.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.410
For more details and
examples, check out

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.180
the expanded blog
posts I've linked

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.730
to down below in
the description.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.079
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:19.620
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.100
and consider subscribing to get
all the latest episodes right

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.300
when they come out.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.060
For now, check out
BigQuery's public datasets,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.630
and start querying away.

